
# ADCS Visualization Dashboard

A web dashboard for real-time visualization of light sensor data and directional light vector in 2D and 3D space. Built using Dash (Plotly), Flask, and Plotly.js with interactive camera handling.

## Project Structure

```
adcs_plot_app/
│
├── app.py                  # Main Dash + Flask application
├── graph.py                # Graph logic and update handling
├── config_app.py           # Global app configuration
├── config_graph.py         # Additional graph configuration
├── camera_interaction.html # Custom Dash HTML template with JS for camera control
├── requirements.txt        # Python dependencies
├── Procfile                # Deployment config for Heroku
```


## Loally Installation

### 1. Clone the repository

```
git clone https://github.com/Stayzie-R/adcs_plot_app.git
cd adcs_plot_app
```


### 2. Create virtual environment and install dependencies
```
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
```

### 3. Run the application locally
```
python3 app.py
```
The app will be locally available at http://127.0.0.1:8050.

## Deployment (Heroku)
This app can be deployed in two main ways:

- **Via GUI (Heroku Dashboard)** – simple and user-friendly, ideal for beginners.
- **Via CLI (Heroku Command Line Interface)** – recommended for more control and automation.

### 1. Deploy via Heroku Dashboard (GUI)
    1. Go to [Heroku Dashboard](#https://dashboard.heroku.com)
    2. Log in or create a new Heroku account
    3. Create a new app via button "New"
    4. Go to Deploy tab
    5. Select GitHub as deployment method in
    6. Connect this GitHub repository
    7. Enable Automatic Deploys or click Deploy Branch
### 2. Deploy via Heroku CLI
For full CLI setup and usage on all operating systems (Windows, macOS, Linux), see the [official Heroku CLI guide](https://devcenter.heroku.com/articles/heroku-cli).

 ## API Endpoint
| Method | Endpoint             | Description                         |
|--------|----------------------|-------------------------------------|
| POST   | `/update_vector`     | Sends light vector and sensor data  |
| POST   | `/interaction_start` | Notifies camera movement started    |
| POST   | `/interaction_end`   | Notifies camera movement stopped    |

## Camera Locking Logic
User interaction with the 3D graph (rotation, zoom) triggers JS events in camera_interaction.html, which:
* Locks the backend update of the 3D graph via `/interaction_start`
* Unlocks it on interaction end via `/interaction_end`
* Prevents overwriting camera view during active interaction

